IsdaYou — Build Checklist (Manuscript-Aligned)

This replaces the previous checklist. Every step below is cross-referenced to the
manuscript requirement it implements, so if a requirement changes later, you know
exactly which build step to revisit.

What changed from the last version, and why:
Training and deployment now cover two models, not one — matching Section 7.3
and MLR-004/005/015/016: YOLO detects and crops the fish; a separate CNN
classifies the species from the cropped region.

Geofencing now runs offline, against locally cached boundary data — matching
the fixed Section 5.3. Only refreshing that data (via the BFAR scraper) needs
internet; checking a user's location against it does not.
Added Android's dedicated Geofencing API, required for FR-016's background
detection — Fused Location Provider alone can't do this.

Terminology corrected to match the manuscript: "closed season" / "restricted
area", not "breeding alerts" / "breeding zone" (those terms appear nowhere in
the manuscript and would confuse a reviewer comparing the two documents).
Python pinned to a safer version range for the ML tooling.

Hybrid Offline/Online Mode — the source of truth for every step below
Offline (no internet) — matches Section 5.3, "Offline Continuity with Periodic Synchronization":
- Fish identification: YOLO (detection) → CNN (classification), both run on-device via TFLite
- Species library lookup: cached `FishSpecies` data in Room
- Restricted-area / closed-season checks: `RestrictedArea.containsLocation()` evaluated against locally cached boundary and date-range data in Room — this is the fix from before
- Catch and identification logging: saved locally in Room, queued for sync

Online (internet available) — matches Section 5.3's sync clause and BR10/FR-024:
- `GovernmentDataSource` scraper refreshes `RestrictedArea` boundaries, closed-season date ranges, and species data from BFAR sources
- `WorkManager` uploads queued `FishImage` / `FishIdentification` logs
- Notifications about newly changed rules can push once refreshed data lands, but the check itself never depends on connectivity
- Keep this distinction in mind through every step — it's the one design point most likely to quietly drift if a step gets written without checking it.


## 1. Set up GitHub (source code storage)
1.1. Create a GitHub account if you don't have one
1.2. Create a private repository named `isdayou`
1.3. Add your teammates as collaborators (Settings → Collaborators)
1.4. Clone the repo locally
1.5. Create three top-level folders:
      `mobile-app/` — Android Studio project
      `backend/` — FastAPI + scraper + PostGIS
      `ml/` — YOLO and CNN training scripts, datasets, exported models
      ---


## 2. Install your coding tools

For the mobile app (Android Client):
2.1. Install Android Studio (bundles Android SDK + emulator)
2.2. Verify JDK is configured (Android Studio ships its own; usually nothing extra to install)
2.3. Set up an emulator at API 28 (Android 9.0), and get at least one real budget Android phone ready for camera/GPS testing — the emulator can't tell you much about either

For the backend, web scrapers, and ML pipeline:
2.4. Install Git
2.5. Install Python 3.11.16 — not the newest release. Ultralytics and TFLite export tooling regularly lag behind the latest Python version; 3.10/3.11 is the safer, 
     better-tested choice for training and export. (The backend/scraper code isn't picky and can share the same environment.)
2.6. Install Node.js LTS — for the web-based admin console only
2.7. Install Docker Desktop — for PostgreSQL + PostGIS
2.8. Install VS Code with the Python and ESLint extensions
