## 3. Find and prepare your Philippine fish dataset
      This step now needs to produce two related but distinct datasets — one for each model.

3.1. Identify target species — prioritize ones with a closed season, size limit, or protected status 
     (matches Section 6.2's species list: Tamban, Alumahan, Hasa-hasa, Galunggong, Danggit, Lapu-lapu, etc., 
     plus common aquaculture species like Bangus and Tilapia if you want easier wins)
3.2. Source images from Roboflow Universe, Kaggle (see the Cabuyao fish-port dataset we already flagged), 
     BFAR references, or your own market photography — no identifiable faces in your own photos
3.3. Build Dataset A — for YOLO (detection): full, uncropped images with a bounding box drawn around each fish. This is what teaches YOLO where the fish is.
3.4. Build Dataset B — for the CNN (classification): once YOLO can detect and crop reliably, run it over your images to produce cropped, 
     per-species-labeled fish-only images. This is what teaches the CNN what species it is. 
     (Early on, before YOLO is trained, you can bootstrap Dataset B with manually cropped images so CNN training can start in parallel.)
3.5. Organize both datasets into folders by species name, kept clearly separate (`/ml/dataset-yolo/` vs `/ml/dataset-cnn/`)
3.6. Build the master CSV/JSON for 
     `FishSpecies` attributes: `commonName`, `scientificName`, `localNames`, `description`, `habitat`, `averageSize`, `conservationStatus`, `minimumSize`, `defaultFishingRestrictions`
     ---


## 4. Train and export the two-stage AI pipeline
4.1 — Train YOLO (detection stage)
      Install: `pip install ultralytics`
      Train YOLOv11 on Dataset A to detect and localize fish 
      (species label at this stage can be coarse or even generic "fish" 
      — YOLO's job here is finding the fish, not naming it)
      Export: `model.export(format="tflite")`
      Apply INT8 quantization for speed and size on budget phones
      Save the exported file as `yolo_detector.tflite`

4.2 — Train the CNN (classification stage)
      Choose a lightweight architecture suited to mobile: MobileNetV3 or EfficientNet-Lite are good starting points — they're built for exactly this kind of on-device constraint
      Train on Dataset B (cropped, species-labeled images) to classify species from an already-localized fish
      Export to TFLite the same way, with INT8 quantization
      Save as `species_classifier.tflite`

4.3 — Generate one shared class-index file
      `labels.txt` maps the CNN's output indices to species names — this is the file MLR-015 refers to when it says predictions must be traceable to a specific trained artifact. 
      Version this file alongside both models; a mismatch here is a silent, dangerous bug (confident predictions with wrong names).

4.4 — Sanity-check the full pipeline before touching Android
      Run a test script on your machine: image in → YOLO detects & crops → CNN classifies → prints result. 
      Confirm this works end-to-end in Python before you spend time wiring it into Kotlin. 
      Debugging two models is much easier outside the app than inside it.

## 5. Set up the backend database (PostgreSQL + PostGIS)
5.1. In `backend/`, configure `docker-compose.yml` with PostgreSQL + PostGIS
5.2. `docker compose up -d`
5.3. Define schemas matching your class diagram: `User`, `FishImage`, `FishIdentification`, `FishSpecies`, `RestrictedArea`, `Location`, `Notification`, `GovernmentDataSource`
5.4. Verify the connection with DBeaver
---

## 6. Build the backend and the government data scraper
6.1. In `backend/`: `python -m venv venv`
6.2. `pip install fastapi uvicorn sqlalchemy psycopg2-binary beautifulsoup4 requests celery`
6.3. Build the scraper (`GovernmentDataSource`) targeting BFAR/DA advisory pages to pull closed-season date ranges (`startDate`, `endDate`) 
     and restricted-area updates — use the manuscript's actual terminology here, not "breeding," so your code, docs, and manuscript all describe the same thing
6.4. Schedule it with APScheduler or Celery, running `GovernmentDataSource.getUpdates()` to refresh `RestrictedArea` records in PostGIS 
     — this is the online-only half of the system; nothing about running the check itself should live here
6.5. Expose sync endpoints: `/api/v1/sync/species`, `/api/v1/sync/geofences`, `/api/v1/notifications`
     ---

## 7. Build the mobile app (Android — Kotlin)
  7.1. In Android Studio: new project in `mobile-app/`, Kotlin, minimum SDK API 28
  7.2. Add dependencies in `build.gradle`:
      CameraX — capturing/selecting a `FishImage`
      TensorFlow Lite Android — runs both `yolo_detector.tflite` and `species_classifier.tflite`
      Room — offline cache for `FishSpecies`, `FishIdentification`, `RestrictedArea` boundaries and closed-season dates, and queued logs
      Retrofit + OkHttp — sync calls to the backend when online
      Play Services Location (Fused Location Provider) — current device coordinates
      Play Services Geofencing API (`GeofencingClient`) — this is the piece the last version was missing. 
        - Location alone only tells you where you are; the Geofencing API is what lets the OS notify your app when a boundary is entered or approached, 
        - including while the app isn't in the foreground, which is what FR-016 actually requires.
      WorkManager — background sync when connectivity returns
      Jetpack Compose — UI
  7.3. Place both `.tflite` files and `labels.txt` in `app/src/main/assets/`
  7.4. Build CameraX capture/gallery-select flow
  7.5. Implement the full local pipeline, matching MLR-001 through MLR-011:
  ```
   Capture image (MLR-001)
     → Validate format/resolution (MLR-002)
     → Preprocess: resize, letterbox, normalize (MLR-003)
     → YOLO detects & localizes fish (MLR-004, MLR-005 — bounding box)
     → Crop detected region
     → CNN classifies species (MLR-005 — class + confidence)
     → Non-max suppression on overlapping detections (MLR-006)
     → Threshold filter (MLR-007) → below threshold: "no confident ID" (MLR-009)
     → Display species, confidence, bounding box overlay (MLR-011), ranked alternatives (MLR-008)
     → Log prediction with both model versions (MLR-013, MLR-015)
   ```
  7.6. Implement `RestrictedArea.containsLocation()` to run locally, against Room's cached boundary and closed-season data, 
     using coordinates from the Fused Location Provider and boundary entry/exit events from the Geofencing API — this must work with the phone in airplane mode
  ---

